{{!--
	Blitzy site footer.

	Renders the wiki's own footer -- the information row, the site-links row and the icons
	row -- inside Blitzy chrome. Every string, link and image inside it is built by
	MediaWiki core. This partial contributes structure and class hooks and nothing else:
	there is no newsletter form, no social-media list, no sitemap, no language switcher and
	no Blitzy-authored link anywhere in it, because rule R12 confines the footer to core's
	own footer data.

	This block comment uses the `--` delimited form on purpose. The plain form ends at the
	first `}}` it meets, so a comment that quotes a template expression -- as the lookup
	note below has to -- terminates early and the rest of the prose is compiled as markup.
	That fails the build with "Unclosed token", which is a good failure, but only after
	someone has spent a while looking for it. Bundled Vector's Logo.mustache and
	Dropdown/Open.mustache use this same delimited form for the same reason.

	CONSUMED TEMPLATE DATA

	  string|null  html-user-language-attributes  Raw attribute string from
	                                              Skin::prepareUserLanguageAttributes().
	  footerRow    data-footer.data-info          The information row.
	  footerRow    data-footer.data-places        The site-links row.
	  footerRow    data-footer.data-icons         The icons row.

	Nothing else is read. Those four names are the complete input surface of this file.

	@typedef object footerRow
	@prop string       id           Element id. Always supplied; emitted verbatim (R2).
	@prop bool         is-empty     True when core supplied the row with no items in it.
	@prop string|null  className    'noprint' on the icons row, null on the other two.
	@prop footerItem[] array-items  The row's items, in the order core built them.

	@typedef object footerItem
	@prop string      id           Element id. Emitted verbatim (R2).
	@prop string      html         Core-built inner HTML for the item.
	@prop string      class        Core's class list for the item. Always carries at least
	                               'mw-list-item'; the icons item also carries 'noprint'.
	@prop string      name         Core's key for the item. Not consumed here.
	@prop array|null  array-links  Structured link data. Not consumed here, because core
	                               has already rendered the same links into 'html'.

	The item ids core supplies, all of which reach the page through this file:
	footer-info-lastmod, footer-info-credits, footer-info-renderedwith,
	footer-info-copyright, footer-places-privacy, footer-places-about,
	footer-places-disclaimers, and footer-<block>ico for each $wgFooterIcons block
	(footer-poweredbyico and footer-copyrightico in a default configuration). The row ids
	are footer-info, footer-places and footer-icons, with no 'p-' prefix, because
	SkinComponentMenu keeps the bare name for any menu whose name starts with 'footer'.

	THREE CORRECTIONS THIS FILE ENCODES

	Each was checked against MediaWiki 1.43.9 -- the release skin.json requires and the
	digest-pinned image the harness runs -- as well as against the 1.47 tree checked out
	beside this package as an API reference. Do not "fix" any of the three into the shape
	the Vector skin on MediaWiki's main branch uses. Every one of those changes leaves a
	footer that still looks plausible in a browser, which is why they are recorded here
	rather than left to be rediscovered.

	  1. The footer rows are read from the footer component, under the `data-footer` key
	     used below, and never from the portlet data. Vector on MediaWiki's main branch
	     reads them from the portlet data instead, through the `footer-info`,
	     `footer-places` and `footer-icons` content-navigation buckets. Those buckets were
	     added in MediaWiki 1.47 (SkinTemplate::buildContentNavigationUrlsInternal(),
	     commented "Added in 1.47: footer menus", T318376; SkinComponentFooter::addItem()
	     is @since 1.47, T426358), and grepping 1.43.9 for their names finds nothing. On
	     the target release Vector's paths therefore resolve to nothing at all: no error,
	     no warning, just an empty footer while every other assertion still passes.
	     `data-footer`, produced by SkinComponentFooter and mapped onto that key by
	     Skin::getTemplateData(), is the contract that is stable on both releases, and it
	     is the documented SkinMustache footer specification. Core's own
	     authentication-popup skin and the Vector build bundled with 1.43 both consume it.

	  2. Each row is assembled by iterating `array-items`, because
	     SkinComponentFooter::formatFooterDataForCurrentSpec() is deliberately lossy: read
	     that method before reaching for any other key. It removes six keys from every row
	     -- the tooltip HTML, the concatenated single-string rendering of the items, the
	     after-portal and before-portal HTML, the menu label and the row's own class -- and
	     removes the pre-rendered list-item HTML from every item, then adds `className`.
	     Only the four row properties and five item properties typed above survive. So the
	     generic portlet shortcuts are unavailable here: there is no one-shot string of
	     items to emit, and no row class, which is exactly why `className` is what carries
	     'noprint' onto the icons row. That same removal is what makes the item-level
	     `{{#class}}` idiom below safe -- see the lookup note further down.

	  3. The copyright and last-modified strings are not read from the `copyright` or
	     `last-modified` components, and the `rendered-with` component is not read at all.
	     Core already places all three strings inside `data-footer.data-info`, as the items
	     footer-info-copyright, footer-info-lastmod and footer-info-renderedwith. Reading
	     the components as well would print each string twice and, worse, emit each of
	     those ids twice. There is in any case no `rendered-with` component to read:
	     SkinComponentRegistry::registerComponents() registers exactly copyright,
	     last-modified, logos, toc, search-box and footer, so asking for it yields silence
	     rather than an error.

	WHY EACH ROW IS GUARDED THREE TIMES

	Every row goes through the same three gates, and each one exists because a measured data
	shape gets past the other two.

	  a. `{{#data-footer.data-<row>.id}}` asks whether core supplied a real row at all. It
	     has to be a gate on the id rather than on the row object, because a row object that
	     describes nothing is still truthy. When $wgFooterIcons holds no usable icon,
	     SkinComponentFooter::getFooterIcons() returns an empty array -- but
	     formatFooterDataForCurrentSpec() then walks every key it was handed and adds
	     `className` regardless, so what actually reaches the template is the single-key
	     object { className: 'noprint' }: no id, no is-empty, no items. A gate on the row
	     object passes it straight through and the row renders as <ul id="">, an empty list
	     with a meaningless id. That was reproduced on a live 1.43.9 instance by emptying
	     $wgFooterIcons, not deduced.

	  b. `{{#data-footer.data-<row>}}` then pushes the row so that everything below reads
	     with relative keys, exactly as core's own footer template does. The gate above
	     cannot double as this push: entering a section on the id makes a bare string the
	     current context, and from inside a string the row's own sibling keys stop
	     resolving -- `className` and `array-items` both come back empty, which silently
	     costs the icons row its `noprint` and every row its items. Also measured.

	  c. `{{^is-empty}}` asks whether the row core supplied holds any items. The information
	     and site-links rows always arrive as fully populated objects, so this is the gate
	     that matters for them: on an edit form, a history list, a diff or a special page
	     there is no revision to date and OutputPage::showsCopyright() is false, so the
	     information row is real but has nothing in it. The Vector build bundled with 1.43
	     renders "<ul id=\"footer-info\">\n</ul>" on all of those pages; this file renders
	     nothing there instead.

	Between them, an absent, placeholder or itemless row emits nothing at all rather than an
	empty <ul>. The three rows then appear in a fixed, unconditional order, so consecutive
	capture runs are byte-identical (R9) and the footer renders at every viewport with
	nothing else conditional anywhere in the file (R11).

	A NOTE ON MUSTACHE LOOKUP, WHICH IS WHY `id` IS INTERPOLATED PLAINLY

	MediaWiki compiles templates with LightnCandy's FLAG_MUSTACHELOOKUP, so a name missing
	from the current frame is searched for in enclosing frames. Inside `array-items` the
	current frame is the item, so `{{id}}` is the item's own id and `{{{html}}}` its own HTML.

	Writing `{{#id}} id="{{id}}"{{/id}}` to make that attribute conditional looks safer and
	is not. Measured against this compiler, it stamps the ROW's id onto every <li>: the
	section makes a bare string the context, the search for `id` skips the frame it came from
	and lands on the row. `{{#id}} id="{{.}}"{{/id}}` fixes that particular mis-resolution
	but not the premise -- an item with no id would still make the section fall back to the
	row's id and duplicate an id the <ul> already carries -- and Mustache mode offers no
	dot-prefixed or `this.`-prefixed form that suppresses the search. So the item id is
	interpolated plainly, the way core's own footer template and Vector both do it, and it is
	correct on every path that supplies footer items on the target release.

	The same string-context behaviour is why the row gate above is a dotted path followed by
	a re-push of the row rather than a `{{#id}}` wrapper around the markup, and why the <ul>
	id is a relative `{{id}}` read from the pushed row rather than a `{{.}}` read from a
	string. The item-level `{{#class}}` carries no risk of its own, because correction 2
	removed the class from the row and no enclosing frame defines one either, so an item
	without a class simply contributes nothing.

	SCOPE OF THIS FILE

	Appearance is entirely the stylesheet's business. There is no `style` attribute, no
	colour value, no directional attribute, no dimension and no message key here: the hooks
	`blitzy-footer`, `blitzy-footer__inner`, `blitzy-footer__row` with its `--info`,
	`--places` and `--icons` modifiers, and `blitzy-footer__item` are the whole contract
	that resources/skins.blitzy.styles/components/Footer.less styles against the tokens
	(R3, R6). The per-row modifiers exist so that stylesheet can reach one row without an id
	selector. Every Blitzy class sits alongside core's ids and classes rather than replacing
	them, and core's item HTML -- including the fake-Codex-button classes core puts on
	footer icon links, which skinStyles/codex.less restyles -- passes through untouched
	(R2, R7). No subresource is requested from here, so the only requests this footer makes
	are the same-origin icon images core builds (R5). The last-edited date inside
	footer-info-lastmod is the single time-bearing string on the page; it is core's own and
	is passed through unreformatted (R9).
--}}
{{!--
	`role` is redundant on <footer> but kept so that gadget and user-style selectors keep
	working and to sidestep a VoiceOver bug. This is the page's only footer, so the landmark
	needs no accessible name, and no heading is introduced here because the page has exactly
	one, core's #firstHeading (R10).
--}}
<footer id="footer" class="mw-footer blitzy-footer" role="contentinfo" {{{html-user-language-attributes}}}>
	<div class="blitzy-footer__inner">
		{{#data-footer.data-info.id}}
		{{#data-footer.data-info}}
		{{^is-empty}}
		<ul id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-footer__row blitzy-footer__row--info{{#className}} {{.}}{{/className}}">
			{{#array-items}}
			<li id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-footer__item{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}">{{{html}}}</li>
			{{/array-items}}
		</ul>
		{{/is-empty}}
		{{/data-footer.data-info}}
		{{/data-footer.data-info.id}}
		{{#data-footer.data-places.id}}
		{{#data-footer.data-places}}
		{{^is-empty}}
		<ul id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-footer__row blitzy-footer__row--places{{#className}} {{.}}{{/className}}">
			{{#array-items}}
			<li id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-footer__item{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}">{{{html}}}</li>
			{{/array-items}}
		</ul>
		{{/is-empty}}
		{{/data-footer.data-places}}
		{{/data-footer.data-places.id}}
		{{#data-footer.data-icons.id}}
		{{#data-footer.data-icons}}
		{{^is-empty}}
		<ul id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-footer__row blitzy-footer__row--icons{{#className}} {{.}}{{/className}}">
			{{#array-items}}
			<li id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-footer__item{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}">{{{html}}}</li>
			{{/array-items}}
		</ul>
		{{/is-empty}}
		{{/data-footer.data-icons}}
		{{/data-footer.data-icons.id}}
	</div>
</footer>
